---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PlaceholderPlate from "../../components/PlaceholderPlate.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../consts";
import { breadcrumbSchema } from "../../lib/schema";

const practice = (await getCollection("practice")).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);

const jsonLd = breadcrumbSchema([
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "Practice", path: "/practice" },
]);

const practiceGallery = [
  { src: "night-workshop-assembly.jpg", alt: "Two craftsmen assembling a walnut and brass reception desk in a night workshop", feature: true },
  { src: "craftsman-arranging-necklace.jpg", alt: "A gloved craftsman arranging a diamond necklace inside a bronze-framed display case", feature: true },
  { src: "workshop-finishing-touches.jpg", alt: "Craftsman annotating construction drawings at a workshop bench, a jewelry display cabinet behind him" },
  { src: "handcrafted-walnut-cabinet.jpg", alt: "Hand-fitting a dovetail joint on a walnut display cabinet" },
  { src: "walnut-bronze-craftsmanship.jpg", alt: "Fitting brass hardware to a walnut cabinet drawer" },
  { src: "tig-welding-display-frame.jpg", alt: "TIG welding the bronze frame of a jewelry display case" },
] as const;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Practice"
  description={`Architectural design, design-build implementation, interior architecture and restoration — the disciplines of ${SITE.name}, based in ${SITE.address.city}, Türkiye.`}
  path="/practice"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="px-6 pt-16 pb-12 md:px-10 md:pt-24">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl">
      <p class="coord-mark">Practice</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 max-w-2xl font-display text-4xl text-ink md:text-6xl">
        Four disciplines, one team.
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-xl text-lg text-ink/75">
        Design and construction stay under one roof, so nothing gets lost between the drawing
        board and the site. <span lang="tr" class="italic">Tasarımdan uygulamaya tek ekip.</span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-stone-light/70 bg-paper px-6 py-16 md:px-10 md:py-24">
    <div class="mx-auto mb-16 max-w-6xl">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-4 md:grid-cols-4 md:auto-rows-[200px] md:grid-flow-dense md:gap-6">
        {
          practiceGallery.map((img) => (
            <img
              src={`/images/practice/${img.src}`}
              alt={img.alt}
              class={`hover-zoom aspect-[4/3] h-full w-full object-cover ${
                "feature" in img && img.feature
                  ? "col-span-2 aspect-auto md:row-span-2"
                  : ""
              }`}
              loading="lazy"
            />
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <p class="mt-6 max-w-2xl font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.08em] text-stone">
        These images are AI-generated — a study of the construction site and workshop, not
        documentary photography.
        <span lang="tr" class="block normal-case not-italic tracking-normal">
          Bu görseller yapay zekâ ile üretilmiştir; şantiye ve atölyeye dair bir çalışmadır,
          belgesel fotoğraf değildir.
        </span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-stone-light/70 px-6 py-12 md:px-10 md:py-20">
    <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-6xl flex-col gap-16 md:gap-24">
      {
        practice.map((discipline, i) => (
          <article class="grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2 md:items-center md:gap-16">
            {
              discipline.data.heroImage ? (
                <div class={`aspect-[4/3] w-full overflow-hidden ${i % 2 === 1 ? "md:order-2" : ""}`}>
                  <img
                    src={discipline.data.heroImage}
                    alt={discipline.data.summary}
                    class="hover-zoom h-full w-full object-cover"
                    loading="lazy"
                  />
                </div>
              ) : (
                <PlaceholderPlate
                  label={discipline.data.title}
                  seed={i + 5}
                  class={`aspect-[4/3] w-full ${i % 2 === 1 ? "md:order-2" : ""}`}
                />
              )
            }
            <div>
              <p class="coord-mark">0{i + 1} — <span lang="tr">{discipline.data.titleTr}</span></p>
              <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-3xl text-ink">{discipline.data.title}</h2>
              <p class="mt-4 text-ink/75">{discipline.data.summary}</p>
              <a
                href={`/practice/${discipline.id}`}
                class="mt-6 inline-block font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-patina hover:underline"
              >
                Read more →
              </a>
            </div>
          </article>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-stone-light/70 bg-paper px-6 py-16 md:px-10 md:py-24">
    <div class="prose-content mx-auto max-w-3xl text-ink/85">
      <p class="coord-mark">Notes on memory</p>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Architectural design is often conceived through drawings, models, and visualizations.
        Yet, a building's true life begins not when design files are finalized, but when
        construction commences on-site. Decisions that appear definitive on paper are reshaped
        when confronted with material behavior, workmanship quality, existing site conditions,
        budget constraints, timeframes, and unforeseen technical issues. Consequently, the
        construction process is not merely the realization of a design but also its first
        encounter with the real world.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every decision made on the construction site leaves a subtle mark on the building's
        future character. An on-site modification to a detail, a variation in material
        application, a craftsman's improvised solution, the way light behaves differently than
        anticipated, or the unexpected weathering of a surface—all become part of the design's
        final form. The building sheds its flawless, abstract state from the drawings and
        transforms into a tangible, material entity. This transformation simultaneously
        establishes the space's initial layer of memory.
      </p>
      <p>However, the true memory of an architectural structure is formed once it comes into use.</p>
      <p>
        When people enter a space, they do not merely use it; they alter it. Where they stand,
        the paths they choose, where they meet, which corners they claim as their own, and how
        they transform the space generate meanings that were never originally envisioned. A
        staircase may cease to be merely a structural element connecting two floors and instead
        become a place where people sit. A square designed as a transit zone may evolve over time
        into a venue for gatherings, protests, celebrations, or simply waiting. The function
        intended by the designer and the meaning created by the user do not always align.
      </p>
      <p>
        The space physically records these modes of use as well. Wear on the floor, the polished
        surface of a door handle, material faded by the sun, a wall constantly touched by people,
        or furniture replaced over the years—these elements make the passage of time within a
        space visible. They are often viewed merely as maintenance issues, yet they are also
        documents of the structure's history.
      </p>
      <p>
        For this reason, it is difficult to pinpoint a definitive moment when architectural
        design is complete. While the design process may technically end when the building is
        handed over, the evolution of the space continues. People, objects, events, and time add
        new layers to the structure.
      </p>
      <p>
        Perhaps good architectural design is not merely that which dictates how a space is used,
        but that which allows users to shape their own experiences. After all, the memory of a
        space cannot be directly designed by the architect; the designer can only create the
        conditions in which such memory can emerge.
      </p>
      <p>Thereafter, the space begins to live.</p>
      <p>
        And as time passes, the structure ceases to be just a designed object; it transforms into
        a repository of memory, bearing the traces left by people, the events that have unfolded,
        and the evolving ways in which it is used.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose-content :global(p) {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .prose-content :global(p:first-child) {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
</style>
